    "q-106983": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106983",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ 4-\\lambda -4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-5)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =25-32 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $Q$ does not have real eigenvalues.",
